From 7e3d9a65fe208576595066301a94c6b3e8ba02c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 05:36:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kv-offload): time disk I/O from the dequeue and by
 progress

Upstream base: the patch applies to local-inference-lab/vllm at
19f2c20ed4d64ae22bd009ceacadd85e2a81e354 after the 702 patches of
spark-ds41f-1120 (candidate-62, 0001 to 0829) and patches 9030 to 9039
of this series.

Rationale: a later read of the code found two gaps. The 15 s timeout of
9033 counted from submission, so a burst of returning sessions queued on
one load thread could degrade the tier with no disk fault. No test
failed when the drain of the load stream after a timeout was removed,
because the host copies of the CPU tests are synchronous. With this
patch the I/O timeout no longer counts queue time. Each IO thread
records when it takes a transfer from the queue and when each slot of
the transfer completes, and a transfer is abandoned when no slot has
completed for disk_io_timeout_s since the later of the two, so a
transfer that keeps completing slots runs to the end. When the tier of
a rank has a fault, every transfer still queued on that rank fails at
once with no I/O, so the loads behind a hung read do not wait for a
blocked thread. The scheduler records the time of each load event and
reports the slowest load since the start as the gauge
vllm:kv_offload_disk_load_seconds_max, which includes the queue time and
the wait for every rank. A debug hook, VLLM_KVTIER_DEBUG_READ_DELAY with
the value <read>:<seconds>[:<rank>], makes one slot read sleep so that a
test or a hardware run can hang a read; it is off by default and is read
once at init.

Quality implications: the patch does not change outputs, and the debug
hook stays off unless the variable is set.

Tests: on the CPU path the offload suite passed 185 tests, with 2
CUDA-only modules skipped. The new file test_ds41_tier_timeouts.py holds
8 tests. In them, 8 loads queued behind each other, the last taking
about 0.96 s under a timeout of 0.3 s, do not degrade the tier, and
neither does one load of 12 slots over 0.72 s. A hung first read fails
its load at the timeout and fails the two loads queued behind it at
once with no I/O. The debug delay is parsed and hangs the named read,
and it is off without the variable. With a load stream whose copies run
0.5 s late, an abandoned load is released only after the copy in flight
lands, and blocks given to another request afterwards keep the new
bytes. The slowest load is reported once the figure rises. A total
bound on a transfer that progresses slowly does not exist at this patch
and was not tested, and neither were the drain on a CUDA stream and the
debug hook on hardware. On DGX Spark hardware the patch ran only as part
of the whole series.

Upstream status: the patch is proposed to spark-ds41f in
experiments/2026-11-22-kv-disk-tier and has not been submitted to
local-inference-lab/vllm.
